window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_c-2f1ccbfc69970f9b"]={"key":"ny_c-2f1ccbfc69970f9b","group":"ny_c","uid":"2f1ccbfc69970f9b","rank":26,"title":"goldfinch franchise owner authority guide","occupation":"pharmacists","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_c1","prompt":"You’re the pharmacist compliance lead for Goldfinch Systems. After a franchise owner used a signature they were not authorized to give on a pharmacy inventory order, Linnea Rutkowski wants a guide in all thirty owners’ hands before the January meeting. Most owners are grocers, not pharmacists, and the stores are split between Minnesota and North Dakota. Owners want operational visibility, while PICs are treating the owner title as either full authority or no authority at all. Estelle Marchetti also needs your recommendation on whether ordering and patient-information access should be enabled by default or only after role approval. Cover the law as of October 1, 2025, with a concise comparison to January 1, 2024. Make clear which requirements are mandatory and which are Goldfinch safeguards; owners must also know what happens when an urgent order cannot be completed because authority or credentials are unavailable. Produce `franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf` of no more than four pages, `state_authority_matrix.xlsx`, and a one-page `authority_exception_card.docx`. Keep patient examples and system click-throughs out. Write all three files into the top level of the working directory using exactly those filenames, and make them usable together without a follow-up call.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf` — Plain-language franchise owner guide covering signing, ordering, information access, state differences, changes over time, and the recommended authority model. (No more than four rendered pages; current through October 1, 2025; compares the applicable position with January 1, 2024; excludes patient examples and software click-through instructions.)\n- `state_authority_matrix.xlsx` — Usable state-by-state authority matrix supporting owner, PIC, controlled-substance ordering, licensing, and information-access decisions. (Must be readable as an operational matrix and remain consistent with the guide and exception card.)\n- `authority_exception_card.docx` — One-page frontline decision aid for situations in which authority, credentials, or permissible access cannot be confirmed. (Exactly one rendered page or less; must provide a safe stop-and-escalate path without software click-through instructions or patient examples.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/authority_exception_card.docx","bytes":39501,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/b303e4f9454a5e95dea0bb399996df67b4d1651fe3c74162d83a68f0537c5ed0.docx","sha256":"b303e4f9454a5e95dea0bb399996df67b4d1651fe3c74162d83a68f0537c5ed0","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"GOLDFINCH SYSTEMS\nAuthority Exception Card\nFrontline stop • verify • escalate • release | Minnesota + North Dakota | current through 10/01/2025\nDEFAULT: Owner ordering authority and patient-level PHI access stay OFF unless role-approved and current authority is confirmed. This is a Goldfinch safeguard; legal requirements below always apply.\n\nSTOP NOW\nDo not sign another person’s name, use a copied signature, or lend/use another person’s CSOS certificate or credentials. Do not proceed on unverified ownership/permit authority. Do not view or disclose PHI without a verified role, purpose, and minimum-necessary basis. Urgency, a deadline, loss, or owner instruction does not bypass these rules.\n\nSituation\n1. Stop & verify\n2. Route\n3. Release only when\nSchedule II order; Form 222; CSOS; POA; local DEA registration\nStop if signer, POA, certificate, registration, or status is expired, revoked, unavailable, or cannot be confirmed. Never recreate under another identity.\nPIC / pharmacy operations; controlled-substance compliance.\nA current local registrant-linked authority and personal credential are verified, or an approved lawful alternative is documented. Delay if needed.\nSchedule III–V order or controlled shipment receipt\nStop if assigned purchaser scope is unknown or receipt/order records do not reconcile.\nPIC / pharmacy operations; controlled-substance compliance for discrepancy.\nAssigned authority is confirmed and pharmacy security, record, receipt, and reconciliation controls are satisfied.\nMN/ND ownership, entity, control, permit, location, or PIC change\nStop signing/closing/implementation when the approved state structure or filing requirement is not verified. ND exceptions/grandfathering are never assumed transferable.\nPharmacy legal / regulatory affairs; Board process as directed.\nCurrent state structure plus required notice, approval, or application status are verified before action.\nPIC conflict or owner instruction about regulated practice\nStop the disputed regulated action. The owner cannot override professional judgment or require a violation, false record, or unauthorized signer.\nPIC, pharmacy operations, compliance/legal as needed.\nA lawful resolution preserves PIC accountability and pharmacist judgment.\nPatient-level PHI request or uncertain purpose\nWithhold access/disclosure. Ownership, curiosity, relationships, disputes, marketing, and general oversight are insufficient.\nPrivacy officer / information security review.\nDocumented workforce role, permitted purpose, minimum-necessary scope and access approval—or another valid disclosure basis—is confirmed.\n\nPreserve, do not copy: requester, requested action, store, time, authority checked, credential/status, document or order identifier, and disposition. Do not copy passwords, private keys, CSOS credentials, signatures, or unnecessary PHI.\nRequired legal rule: Use only verified current authority.  Goldfinch safeguard: retain the concise exception record and revalidate changed roles or credentials promptly.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}},{"name":"package/private/gold/franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","bytes":14238,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/25884744caf98cca7ddb0f02bd9e0545607d51d79bc03fd995a41e2121bf1300.pdf","sha256":"25884744caf98cca7ddb0f02bd9e0545607d51d79bc03fd995a41e2121bf1300","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"GOLDFINCH SYSTEMS | FRANCHISE AUTHORITY                                                                                                                  Operational guide • current through 01 Oct 2025\n\n\n\n\n                                           Franchise Owner Authority Guide\n  Minnesota + North Dakota retail pharmacies | Federal controlled-substance and HIPAA baseline | Current through 1 October\n  2025\n\n   DECISION: Default owner ordering authority and patient-level access to OFF. Enable only after role approval, current\n   documentation, and the applicable regulated authority are confirmed. This is a Goldfinch safeguard; it does not replace legal\n   requirements.\n\n\n  The simple rule\n  LAW — must: A franchise, corporate title, check-signing authority, or funding responsibility does not itself make someone the pharmacy license/permit\n  holder, DEA registrant, pharmacist-in-charge (PIC), pharmacist, authorized controlled-substance signer, workforce member, or authorized PHI user.\n  Urgency, a vendor deadline, inventory pressure, financial ownership, or an owner instruction cannot cure missing authority.\n  Role                                       What it is — and is not\n\n  Franchise owner / entity officer           Business authority only. May sign a truthful owner/applicant or entity submission if that form calls for it and the signer is authorized for the entity; may\n                                             not sign a PIC, pharmacist, DEA-registrant, or authorized-purchaser attestation merely because of ownership.\n\n  Licensed pharmacy / permit holder          The approved entity and location to which state licensure/permit attaches. A franchise relationship or another store’s permit is not the local license.\n\n  DEA registrant                             The registered pharmacy location for controlled substances. Each location must have the applicable registration; another store’s registration is not\n                                             portable.\n\n  PIC / pharmacist                           PIC remains accountable for lawful practice, supervision, security, records, and operation. Delegation of a business task does not transfer\n                                             professional judgment or regulatory accountability.\n\n  Authorized purchaser / signer              A person with the specific authority required for that transaction; title is not enough. Controlled-substance authority is checked against the registrant,\n                                             POA, and personal credential where applicable.\n\n  Workforce member / business                A workforce member may use PHI only for an assigned permitted function and minimum necessary amount. A separate service provider is a business\n  associate                                  associate only under the required relationship; a BAA is not permission to browse.\n\n\n  Owner / PIC boundary\n  LAW — must: An owner may not direct the PIC or another pharmacist to violate law, override professional judgment, falsify records, or use an\n  unauthorized signer. The PIC is not the owner’s purchasing delegate. GOLDFINCH SAFEGUARD: document the assigned function, entity role, approver,\n  scope, and end date before enabling any exception.\n\n\n\n\nInternal compliance resource — not a substitute for regulator, counsel, or board review for a specific transaction.                                                                                  Page 1\n\fGOLDFINCH SYSTEMS | FRANCHISE AUTHORITY                                                                                                               Operational guide • current through 01 Oct 2025\n\n\n\n  Controlled-substance ordering & receipt\n  Never: sign another person’s name; use or lend another person’s CSOS certificate/credential; treat a copied signature as authorization; or recreate an\n  order under an unavailable person’s identity. These are stop conditions.\n  Transaction                     Legal boundary — must                                                                               Goldfinch operating control\n\n  Schedule I–II order             DEA Form 222 applies to Schedule I or II orders. In retail practice, Schedule I is ordinarily not   Keep a current signer/POA roster by DEA-registered location;\n                                  ordered; Schedule II ordering still requires the registrant or a person holding the registrant’s    independently verify before activation and revalidate\n                                  valid DEA power of attorney (POA) before signing. An ordinary purchase-order signature is           periodically.\n                                  insufficient.\n\n  CSOS order                      CSOS is the electronic controlled-substance ordering method. It does not bypass                     Disable access promptly when employment, role,\n                                  registration-linked authority or Form 222 concepts. The certificate is individual—not a shared      authorization, registration, certificate, or relevant status ends\n                                  store credential—and must be current for the authorized signer.                                     or changes.\n\n  Schedule III–V order            Form 222/CSOS is not generally the ordering instrument for Schedule III–V. They remain              Use assigned purchasing authority; do not treat the owner’s\n                                  controlled substances: use the properly registered pharmacy’s security, records, and                ordinary grocery purchasing authority as a drug-inventory\n                                  authorized-purchasing controls.                                                                     authorization.\n\n  Noncontrolled business          Ordinary business purchasing may be signed under entity authority. Prescription-drug                Separate general procurement permissions from pharmacy\n  goods                           purchasing still must be for the licensed entity and within assigned authority; it is not           inventory permissions.\n                                  professional authority.\n\n  Receipt / discrepancy           The pharmacy must maintain required controlled-substance receipt, reconciliation, security, and     Match shipment to authorized order records; secure product\n                                  record controls. Owner involvement never replaces them.                                             and escalate a discrepancy to PIC and controlled-substance\n                                                                                                                                      compliance.\n\n\n  If authority is unavailable\n  LAW — must: stop the regulated action until current authority is confirmed. Do not “borrow” a signature, credential, registration, or other store’s DEA\n  authority. Escalate: PIC/pharmacy operations for immediate practice and inventory controls; controlled-substance compliance for Form 222, CSOS, POA,\n  registration, or alternate-supply questions. A currently authorized person may act only within current scope. A lawful transfer, alternate supplier process,\n  or delay may be considered only when permitted and approved—not promised in every circumstance.\n\n  POA lifecycle\n  LAW — must: controlled-substance POA is a documented grant from the DEA registrant, retained with required records, revocable, and limited to its\n  scope. Check it; do not infer it from job title. GOLDFINCH SAFEGUARD: retain a status check showing grant, scope, location, revocation/termination\n  review, and reviewer.\n\n\n\n\nInternal compliance resource — not a substitute for regulator, counsel, or board review for a specific transaction.                                                                                Page 2\n\fGOLDFINCH SYSTEMS | FRANCHISE AUTHORITY                                                                                                                   Operational guide • current through 01 Oct 2025\n\n\n\n  State structure, ownership & professional accountability\n  Minnesota                                                                                                   North Dakota\n\n  LAW — must: Nonpharmacist ownership can be part of a lawful structure, but it does not                      LAW — must: Pharmacist-ownership restrictions apply. A new nonpharmacist grocery\n  confer professional, DEA, or PHI authority. The pharmacy must remain licensed, have an                      owner cannot assume a franchise agreement authorizes pharmacy ownership or operation.\n  appropriately licensed PIC, and meet applicable ownership/applicant disclosure and                          Any statutory exception, grandfathered arrangement, or board-approved structure is\n  operational requirements. Confirm state action before a location, owner/entity, control, or                 entity-specific and nontransferable without verification. Before any\n  PIC change; it may require notice, approval, or a new application.                                          ownership/control/entity/PIC change, obtain Board/legal review of the approved structure\n                                                                                                              and required filing/approval.\n\n\n   TIME CHECK — 1 Jan 2024 → 1 Oct 2025: The reviewed core rules on DEA location/signer authority, Form 222/CSOS/POA, HIPAA\n   role-based minimum-necessary access, PIC accountability, Minnesota nonpharmacist ownership posture, and North Dakota\n   pharmacist-ownership restrictions remained materially stable for this guide. The post-signature-incident default-off workflow,\n   independent verification, revalidation, and exception record are Goldfinch policy clarifications, not claimed changes in law.\n\n\n  Patient information: visibility without browsing\n  LAW — must: Ownership does not create a right to view patient names, prescriptions, profiles, claims, counseling information, or other PHI. Internal\n  access requires covered-entity workforce status, an assigned permissible pharmacy/health-care function, authorization, and the minimum amount\n  needed. Access must be controlled and auditable; suspected unauthorized access is reported promptly. Disclosure to an outside owner, adviser, affiliate,\n  or service provider needs an appropriate legal basis. A business-associate relationship is service-limited, not blanket browsing permission.\n  Not sufficient by itself: curiosity; employee, family, or personal relationship; a dispute; marketing; general owner oversight; or confidentiality promise.\n  GOLDFINCH SAFEGUARD: meet ordinary owner oversight with aggregate, de-identified, limited, or otherwise role-appropriate business reporting.\n  Patient-level access is a documented, role-approved exception only when the function and minimum necessary scope are established.\n\n  If the purpose or authority is unclear\n  LAW — must: withhold access or disclosure; do not decide a novel privacy question at the counter. Preserve only needed request facts, then route to\n  privacy/security review. Resume only after documented role/purpose/access approval or another valid legal basis. GOLDFINCH SAFEGUARD: retain a\n  concise exception record without PHI beyond what review needs.\n\n\n\n\nInternal compliance resource — not a substitute for regulator, counsel, or board review for a specific transaction.                                                                               Page 3\n\fGOLDFINCH SYSTEMS | FRANCHISE AUTHORITY                                                                                                                     Operational guide • current through 01 Oct 2025\n\n\n\n  Use this operating model\n  1 | Identify the action                                                 2 | Confirm the authority                                         3 | Act or stop\n\n  Is it entity business, pharmacy/permit, Schedule II,                    Check local license/permit; local DEA registration;               If current authority is verified, proceed within scope. If\n  Schedule III–V, receipt, PIC/professional practice, or PHI?             POA/signer and personal CSOS status; assigned                     unavailable, expired, revoked, unverified, or disputed: stop,\n                                                                          workforce role/purpose/minimum necessary; or state                preserve limited facts, route to the listed function, and\n                                                                          ownership approval, as applicable.                                release only on objective confirmation or approved lawful\n                                                                                                                                            alternative.\n\n\n\n  Escalation routes & release conditions\n  Question / stop                                                     Route                                                     Release only when\n\n  Form 222, CSOS, POA, DEA registration,                              PIC / pharmacy operations, then controlled-substance      Current registrant-linked authority and personal credential are verified,\n  controlled-substance order or receipt                               compliance                                                or the responsible function documents a lawful alternative.\n\n  Owner/entity/control/PIC/permit status, especially North            Pharmacy legal / regulatory affairs and state Board       The applicable state structure and filing/approval status are verified\n  Dakota                                                              review as directed                                        before closing or implementation.\n\n  PIC conflict or instruction contrary to practice law                PIC, pharmacy operations, and compliance/legal as         A lawful resolution preserves the PIC’s professional judgment and\n                                                                      needed                                                    accountability.\n\n  PHI request, access, or disclosure purpose uncertain                Privacy officer / information security review             Documented role, purpose, minimum-necessary scope and access\n                                                                                                                                approval, or other valid disclosure basis.\n\n\n  Authority base\n  Federal: 21 U.S.C. §822; 21 CFR §§1301.12, 1305.03, 1305.05, 1305.21–.28; HIPAA Privacy/Security Rules, 45 CFR §§164.502, .504(e), .514(d), .308(a)(3), .312. Minnesota:\n  Minn. Stat. ch. 151 (including §151.252) and Minn. R. ch. 6800. North Dakota: N.D.C.C. ch. 43-15 (including §43-15-35) and N.D. Admin. Code tit. 61. Confirm current Board and\n  DEA instructions for a specific filing or transaction.\n  Companion tools: State Authority Matrix (action-by-state conditions and sources) and Authority Exception Card (immediate stop/verify/escalate/release).\n\n\n\n\nInternal compliance resource — not a substitute for regulator, counsel, or board review for a specific transaction.                                                                                  Page 4\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}},{"name":"package/private/gold/state_authority_matrix.xlsx","bytes":9610,"extension":".xlsx","url":"assets/b27ddc143c42d0a03a20f83b462975545408b2264a72ce7270830116f7d637ed.xlsx","sha256":"b27ddc143c42d0a03a20f83b462975545408b2264a72ce7270830116f7d637ed","preview":{"kind":"sheets","sheets":[{"name":"Authority Matrix","row_count":15,"rows":[{"r":"1","cells":["Goldfinch Systems | State Authority Matrix"]},{"r":"2","cells":["Minnesota + North Dakota retail pharmacies | Federal baseline | Current through 10/01/2025 | Default: owner ordering authority and patient-level PHI access OFF until role-approved."]},{"r":"4","cells":["Action","Federal / baseline — must","Minnesota result","North Dakota result","Who may act / condition","Goldfinch safeguard","If not confirmed","Authority / effective date"]},{"r":"5","cells":["Pharmacy ownership / permit status","State structure controls; ownership is not pharmacy-practice, DEA, or PHI authority.","Nonpharmacist ownership may be lawful; pharmacy license, PIC, disclosures and operating requirements still apply. Confirm action before owner/entity/control/location/PIC changes.","Pharmacist-ownership restrictions apply. Franchise or grocery acquisition alone does not authorize ownership/operation. Any exception, grandfathering, or Board approval is entity-specific; verify before any change.","Approved licensed/permit entity; proper applicant/owner signature only if the form calls for it and signer has entity authority.","Pre-close state-structure review and documented approval path.","Stop closing/implementation; route pharmacy legal/regulatory affairs and Board process.","Minn. Stat. ch.151 incl. §151.252; Minn. R. ch.6800. N.D.C.C. ch.43-15 incl. §43-15-35; N.D. Admin. Code tit.61. Reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"6","cells":["State application signatures","Truthful entity/owner signature is distinct from professional/registrant attestation.","Owner/entity representative may sign only applicant/owner fields within entity authority; PIC/pharmacist attestations stay with the designated professional.","Same signature boundary; also verify eligible structure before signing ownership/permit documents.","Signer named by form and authorized for the entity; professional/PIC/registrant fields only by the required role.","Legal/compliance review of change filings.","Hold submission and verify form role, entity authority, and state status.","State sources above; reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"7","cells":["Noncontrolled purchasing","Ordinary business purchasing is not controlled-substance or professional authority; prescription-drug purchase must be for licensed entity and assigned role.","Same.","Same.","Entity-authorized purchaser acting for local licensed pharmacy when drug inventory involved.","Separate general procurement from pharmacy inventory permissions.","PIC/pharmacy operations confirms purchaser scope.","State pharmacy licensure baseline; reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"8","cells":["Schedule II ordering","Form 222 is for Schedule I–II. Retail pharmacies ordinarily do not order Schedule I. Each CS location requires applicable DEA registration; another store registration cannot substitute.","Same federal result.","Same federal result.","DEA registrant or person with registrant’s valid, current POA before signing; local registration only.","Current location-specific signer/POA roster; independent verification.","Stop; controlled-substance compliance and PIC assess currently authorized signer/lawful alternative/delay.","21 USC §822; 21 CFR §§1301.12, 1305.03, .05. Reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"9","cells":["Schedule III–V ordering","Form 222/CSOS not generally the ordering instrument; controlled-substance registration, security, records, and authorized purchasing still apply.","Same federal result.","Same federal result.","Assigned purchaser for properly registered pharmacy, within vendor/state controls.","Separate Schedules III–V permissions from general purchasing.","Stop and confirm controlled-substance purchaser authority.","21 CFR Parts 1301, 1304, 1305; reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"10","cells":["CSOS use","Electronic controlled-substance ordering; does not bypass registrant-linked authority. Certificate is individual, current, and never shared.","Same federal result.","Same federal result.","Authorized person using own valid CSOS certificate for registered location and scope.","Deactivate upon role/employment/certificate/authorization change; periodic revalidation.","Do not use another person’s credential; route CS compliance.","21 CFR §§1305.21–.28; reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"11","cells":["Controlled-substance power of attorney","POA must be documented by registrant, in effect before use, retained, revocable, and checked for scope/status.","Same federal result.","Same federal result.","Only registrant or current POA holder; job title is insufficient.","Track location, scope, grant, revocation, termination review.","No POA found or status uncertain = no signature; escalate CS compliance.","21 CFR §1305.05; reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"12","cells":["PIC decisions / professional practice","Business tasks do not move PIC accountability or pharmacist professional judgment.","Licensed PIC accountable under state law; owner may not direct violation, records falsification, or override judgment.","Same; ownership rule adds no power over professional decisions.","PIC/pharmacist for professional practice; owner cannot compel unlawful act.","Escalate a PIC-owner conflict to pharmacy operations/compliance/legal.","Stop disputed regulated action; preserve facts, route PIC + compliance.","Minn. Stat. ch.151; Minn. R. ch.6800; N.D.C.C. ch.43-15; N.D. Admin. Code tit.61. Reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"13","cells":["Patient-level PHI","Ownership creates no automatic viewing right. Internal use requires workforce authorization, assigned permissible function, minimum necessary; controls/auditability and prompt reporting apply. External disclosure requires appropriate legal basis.","Same HIPAA result.","Same HIPAA result.","Approved workforce role with permitted function/minimum necessary; BA access only for contracted function, never blanket browsing.","Default patient-level access OFF; use documented role approval and review.","Withhold access/disclosure; preserve limited request facts; privacy/security review.","45 CFR §§164.502, .504(e), .514(d), .308(a)(3), .312. Reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"14","cells":["Business reporting","Use/disclose only as permitted; minimum necessary applies where applicable.","Same.","Same.","Owner visibility through aggregate, de-identified, limited, or otherwise role-appropriate reporting.","Prefer minimized reporting over patient-level detail.","Privacy review if report purpose/recipient/scope is unclear.","45 CFR §§164.502, .514; reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"15","cells":["Exception escalation","Urgency, vendor deadline, financial ownership, or owner instruction cannot bypass legal authority.","Same; state action may be needed for change questions.","Same; verify ownership/exception/approval before change.","PIC/pharmacy ops; CS compliance; privacy/security; or legal/regulatory based on issue.","Concise exception record: requester, action, store, time, authority checked, status, record/order ID, disposition; no credentials/unneeded PHI.","Resume only on verified current authority, documented privacy approval, or approved lawful alternative.","Federal/state sources above; reviewed 10/01/2025"]},{"r":"17","cells":["Reading note: “must” denotes legal/regulatory requirements. “Goldfinch safeguard” denotes enterprise control. Each row is separately filterable; state differences are deliberately retained."]}],"truncated":false}],"note":"值与公式提取，不还原原始排版；每表最多120行、30列。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"All three required files exist at the top level under the exact requested filenames and open successfully in their native formats.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"The submitted package.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filenames, verify that no file is nested in a subdirectory, and open or parse each artifact. A renamed, corrupt, or format-mismatched artifact does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered owner guide is no more than four pages.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Count rendered PDF pages, including title pages, source notes, appendices, and intentionally blank pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered exception card occupies no more than one page and remains legible without clipped or overlapping content.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX using a standard office renderer and inspect the resulting page count and legibility."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The state matrix is a functional spreadsheet whose operative cells are readable and whose state/action records can be sorted or filtered without destroying meaning.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Open the workbook and verify that the decision content is stored as cells rather than a pasted image, that headers remain associated with records, and that ordinary sorting or filtering is practical."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package identifies Minnesota, North Dakota, and applicable federal law as its scope and states that the assessment is current through October 1, 2025.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit jurisdictional scope and as-of date in the guide and a compatible scope or effective-date field in the matrix."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide compares the relevant authority position at January 1, 2024 with the position at October 1, 2025 rather than merely displaying both dates.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Credit a concise comparison that states which owner-authority domains materially changed or remained unchanged and what that means operationally."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The period comparison distinguishes legal or regulatory changes from Goldfinch policy clarifications prompted by the signature incident and does not invent a legal change merely to populate the comparison.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Check that a change in internal controls is labeled as such and that any claimed change in law is supported by an accurate authority and effective date. A defensible statement that a reviewed domain did not materially change is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package makes one clear recommendation for the default operating model governing owner ordering authority and access to patient information.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"The recommendation must resolve default-enabled versus role-approved access and authority. It may allow documented exceptions, but it cannot leave the default undecided."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommended model is justified by the separation among ownership, pharmacy or DEA registration, PIC accountability, assigned job duties, and minimum-necessary privacy access.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Look for a concise rationale tied to the actual compliance mechanics rather than an unsupported preference for tighter or looser access."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Legally mandatory requirements and Goldfinch safeguards are visibly and consistently differentiated.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"All three deliverables.","grader_hint":"Accept clear labels such as must, law, required, Goldfinch control, or recommended safeguard. Do not credit vague advice that leaves an owner unable to tell whether a statement is legally compulsory."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide distinguishes the franchise owner, pharmacy permit or license holder, DEA registrant, pharmacist-in-charge, pharmacist, authorized purchaser or signer, workforce member, and business associate where relevant.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"The terms need not appear in a glossary, but their separate legal and operational functions must be understandable and must not be collapsed into a single concept of owner authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide clearly states that franchise ownership, corporate title, check-signing authority, or funding responsibility does not by itself confer pharmacy-professional, controlled-substance ordering, or PHI-access authority.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Look for an affirmative separation of business authority from regulated authority, not merely a warning to be careful."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide explains that pharmacy licensure or permitting attaches to the licensed pharmacy, approved owner or operating entity, and location, and that franchise affiliation alone is not the license.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Credit language that prevents an owner from treating a system-wide franchise relationship or another store's authority as transferable to the local pharmacy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide explains that each retail pharmacy location handling controlled substances must operate under the appropriate DEA registration and that another store's registration cannot be used as a substitute.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Check for the separate-registration or location principle and ensure the matrix does not imply system-wide or owner-wide DEA authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package accurately limits DEA Form 222 to ordering Schedule I or II controlled substances and applies the retail-pharmacy context without implying that an ordinary purchase-order signature is sufficient.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"The guide should make Schedule II ordering understandable to an owner while preserving the Form 222 authority requirement. Retail pharmacies ordinarily do not order Schedule I products, but the legal scope may still be described accurately."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"CSOS is accurately presented as an electronic method for controlled-substance orders that still requires valid registration-linked authority and an individual certificate rather than a shared store credential.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Credit only if electronic ordering is not described as bypassing Form 222 authority concepts and the digital credential is treated as personal to the certificate holder."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide explains that a person signing DEA Form 222 orders must be the registrant or hold a valid power of attorney executed under DEA requirements before signing.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Look for the before-signing sequence and for authority based on the DEA registrant's power of attorney, not merely permission from a franchise owner, manager, or PIC."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package describes controlled-substance power of attorney as a documented, retained, and revocable grant whose scope must be checked rather than assumed from job title.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"The operational explanation should support confirming the grant and its current status. It need not reproduce a power-of-attorney form."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package unambiguously prohibits signing another person's name, lending or using another person's CSOS certificate or credentials, or treating a copied signature as authorization.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"All three deliverables.","grader_hint":"Verify that the prohibition is direct enough for an owner or frontline employee to apply during an urgent order and appears in the exception card as an immediate stop condition."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide addresses revocation or loss of ordering authority when employment, role, authorization, registration, certificate, or other relevant status ends or changes.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Look for a requirement to stop use and update or revoke access promptly rather than allowing authority to continue until a periodic review."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide correctly distinguishes Schedule III–V ordering from Schedule II ordering without implying that Schedule III–V products are unregulated or may be ordered outside the pharmacy registrant's controls.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Credit an explanation that Form 222 or its CSOS equivalent is not generally the ordering instrument for Schedule III–V products while controlled-substance registration, security, record, and authorized purchasing controls still apply."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package distinguishes ordinary noncontrolled business purchasing from prescription-drug and controlled-substance authority while still requiring the purchaser to act for the properly licensed entity and within assigned authority.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"The guide should not imply either that every grocery purchase needs pharmacist authority or that an owner's ordinary purchasing power automatically extends to regulated drug inventory."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide addresses receipt accountability for controlled substances, including checking the shipment against authorized order records and escalating discrepancies rather than allowing owner involvement to replace pharmacy controls.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Look for an operational distinction between placing an order and the pharmacy's controlled-substance receipt, reconciliation, recordkeeping, and discrepancy process."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package explains that the PIC is accountable for pharmacy practice compliance, supervision, security, records, and operation under applicable pharmacy law even when business tasks are delegated.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Do not require those exact nouns, but the document must preserve the PIC's regulatory accountability and not portray the PIC as merely an owner's purchasing delegate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide states that an owner may not direct the PIC or another pharmacist to violate pharmacy law, override professional judgment, falsify records, or use an unauthorized signer.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Credit a direct boundary on owner instructions. General language about cooperation is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The North Dakota guidance accurately identifies the state's pharmacist-ownership restrictions and does not tell a new nonpharmacist grocery owner that a franchise agreement alone permits ownership or operation of the pharmacy.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Review against North Dakota pharmacy ownership law in effect on October 1, 2025. The guidance should direct attention to the legally approved ownership or operating structure before the owner signs ownership or permit documents."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The North Dakota guidance qualifies any exception, grandfathered arrangement, or entity-specific approval and requires verification before an ownership, control, or entity change rather than presenting an exception as generally transferable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Credit careful treatment of legacy or statutory exceptions. The package must not imply that buying a franchised grocery store automatically inherits a pharmacy ownership exception."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The Minnesota guidance accurately explains that nonpharmacist ownership does not itself provide professional authority and remains subject to pharmacy licensure, an appropriately licensed PIC, and applicable disclosure and operational requirements.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"The matrix should distinguish Minnesota's ownership posture from North Dakota's without translating Minnesota's more permissive ownership framework into unrestricted pharmacy-practice authority."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package warns that ownership, legal entity, control, location, or PIC changes may trigger advance notice, board approval, a new application, or other state-specific action that must be confirmed before closing or implementation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Look for a pre-change verification boundary rather than a universal claim that one filing rule applies identically in both states."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For each owner action represented in the matrix, the Minnesota and North Dakota entries either state the applicable difference or expressly show that the result is the same; state-dependent ownership rules are not merged into one national answer.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Walk the matrix action by action. A shared federal rule may produce the same result, but ownership and state-license questions must preserve the relevant state distinction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package states that ownership does not create an automatic right to view patient names, prescriptions, profiles, claims, counseling information, or other PHI.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"All three deliverables.","grader_hint":"The rule must be stated directly and should be visible in both the main explanation and the frontline decision path."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Internal PHI access is tied to an assigned role, a permissible pharmacy or health-care function, workforce authorization, and the minimum amount needed for that role.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Credit only if the guide links access to actual duties and minimum-necessary controls rather than relying on employment, ownership, curiosity, or a general confidentiality promise."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide correctly distinguishes an owner acting within the covered entity's workforce from a separate person or entity performing services as a business associate and does not treat a business associate agreement as blanket permission to browse PHI.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Look for role- and service-limited access in either status. A business associate agreement should not be presented as the cure for access unrelated to contracted functions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide provides a practical path for legitimate owner visibility through aggregate, de-identified, limited, or otherwise role-appropriate business reporting instead of assuming patient-level access is necessary.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"Credit an operational alternative that lets owners oversee business performance while minimizing patient information. The document need not prescribe a particular software report."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package identifies curiosity, employee or family relationships, disputes, marketing, and general owner oversight as insufficient reasons to access a patient's record without a separate permitted basis.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf or authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"Equivalent concise examples are acceptable, but the material must make clear that personal interest and broad business status are not treatment, payment, or health-care operations purposes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The guide distinguishes internal role-based access from disclosure of PHI to an outside owner, adviser, affiliate, or service provider and requires an appropriate legal basis for the latter.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","grader_hint":"The document must not describe all owner viewing as internal access. Look for recognition that entity and workforce status affect whether information is being used internally or disclosed externally."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package states that PHI access is subject to access controls, logging or auditability, and prompt reporting of suspected unauthorized access.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"The guide should address both prevention and response without including system-specific click instructions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"When no authorized Schedule II signer or valid CSOS certificate is available, the package directs staff not to complete or recreate the controlled-substance order under someone else's identity and provides a lawful escalation or alternate-supply path.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"The path may include contacting the PIC or compliance function, using another currently authorized signer, arranging a lawful transfer or alternate supplier process where permitted, or delaying the order. It must not promise that every alternative is legally available in every circumstance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception path separately addresses expired, revoked, unavailable, or unverifiable authority and requires current confirmation before work resumes.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"Look for a stop, verification, escalation, and release sequence. Merely telling staff to ask a manager is insufficient if the manager's authority is also unknown."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"When the purpose or authority for PHI access is uncertain, the package directs staff to withhold access or disclosure, preserve the request details, and escalate for privacy review.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"The path must work for an owner request made at the counter and should not tell frontline staff to decide a novel privacy question by themselves."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Urgency, inventory pressure, financial ownership, a vendor deadline, or an owner's instruction is expressly treated as insufficient to bypass controlled-substance, licensing, or privacy authority requirements.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf or authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"Credit a direct no-bypass rule that applies to the practical pressures likely to arise when the normal procedure cannot be completed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception card routes controlled-substance ordering, state ownership or permit questions, PIC conflicts, and PHI questions to appropriately different functions or role-holders.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"Named individuals and phone numbers are not required, but the card must distinguish routes such as PIC or pharmacy operations, controlled-substance compliance, board or legal review, and privacy or security review."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception path tells the frontline user what facts and records to preserve without copying unnecessary PHI or controlled-substance credentials.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"Useful facts include requester, requested action, store, time, authority checked, credential status, document or order identifier, and disposition. Patient detail should be limited to what the escalation genuinely requires."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception card gives immediate, unmistakable stop conditions for unauthorized signatures, shared credentials, unverified ownership or permit authority, and unjustified PHI access.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"A frontline reader should be able to identify these conditions at a glance and know not to proceed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The exception card states objective release conditions for resuming the action, such as verified current authority, an approved lawful alternative, or documented privacy approval.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"authority_exception_card.docx","grader_hint":"The card must not leave the action suspended indefinitely or allow the same requester to self-approve. The release condition should correspond to the reason for the stop."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The state matrix covers, at minimum, pharmacy ownership or permit status, state application signatures, noncontrolled purchasing, Schedule II ordering, Schedule III–V ordering, CSOS use, controlled-substance power of attorney, PIC decisions, patient-level PHI, business reporting, and exception escalation for both states.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Count an action as covered only if the matrix gives a usable result or condition for both Minnesota and North Dakota. Equivalent action names are acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The matrix and guide identify credible controlling authorities and effective dates for the federal DEA, HIPAA, Minnesota, and North Dakota propositions without burdening owners with unexplained citation text.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Validate material propositions against authorities in force on October 1, 2025. Expected source families include the Controlled Substances Act and 21 CFR Parts 1301 and 1305, the HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules, Minnesota pharmacy statutes and rules, and North Dakota pharmacy statutes and administrative rules. Pinpoint references or links are helpful but must be accurate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every legal result or condition stated in the exception card and state matrix agrees with the fuller explanation in the owner guide.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"All three deliverables.","grader_hint":"Cross-check shared topics, especially North Dakota ownership, Minnesota owner authority, Form 222, CSOS, power of attorney, PIC boundaries, PHI access, and stop conditions. Simplification is acceptable only if it does not reverse or materially broaden the rule."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommended default model and the must-versus-safeguard labels remain consistent across the guide, matrix, and exception card.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"All three deliverables.","grader_hint":"A control labeled as Goldfinch policy in one file must not be presented as statutory law in another, and no quick-reference instruction may silently enable authority that the guide makes exception-based."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package is specific, plain-language, visually navigable, and usable by a nonpharmacist owner or frontline employee without asking the author a follow-up question, while minimizing sensitive information.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"All three deliverables.","grader_hint":"Assess the package as an operational whole. It should define unavoidable technical terms, use decisive action language, make state differences easy to find, avoid unexplained legal quotations, contain no patient examples or unnecessary PHI, and provide enough context to act safely."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package distinguishes owner or entity signatures on truthful business and licensing submissions from attestations reserved to the PIC, pharmacist, DEA registrant, or specifically authorized signer.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":true,"evidence":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf and state_authority_matrix.xlsx","grader_hint":"Look for a usable boundary: an owner may sign a document only when the form calls for the owner or applicant and the signer has entity authority, but may not sign professional, registrant, or PIC attestations merely because the owner controls the business."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Goldfinch Systems operates a multistate retail-pharmacy franchise network whose nonpharmacist owners need to act without confusing business ownership, pharmacy licensure, DEA authority, professional accountability, or access to protected health information. The package will be used by owners and frontline pharmacy personnel in decisions carrying controlled-substance, privacy, licensure, and patient-safety consequences.","difficulty_sources":["Translating controlled-substance ordering authority into plain language without falsely stating that nonpharmacist owners can never participate","Separating pharmacy ownership, entity authority, DEA registration, PIC accountability, and individual signer credentials","Reconciling Minnesota and North Dakota lay-ownership rules without assuming entity-specific exceptions","Drawing a visible boundary between legal mandates and prudent enterprise safeguards","Providing a safe and usable path when authority or credentials cannot be confirmed","Keeping three dependent artifacts legally and operationally consistent under tight page limits"],"authoring_plan":{"mode":"instructional","solver_instance":"A retail-pharmacy compliance pharmacist must convert federal controlled-substance rules, HIPAA role-based access requirements, and materially different Minnesota and North Dakota pharmacy ownership rules into three dependent tools for nonpharmacist owners and frontline staff.","intended_verdict":"Recommend an exception-based, role-approved model: ownership alone enables neither controlled-substance ordering authority nor patient-level information access. Business visibility should ordinarily be met with aggregate, de-identified, limited, or otherwise role-appropriate reporting. Any ordering or PHI access granted to an owner must arise from a genuine assigned function, current documentation, and the applicable regulated authority.","legal_scope":{"as_of_date":"2025-10-01","comparison_date":"2024-01-01","jurisdictions":["Federal controlled-substance and HIPAA requirements","Minnesota","North Dakota"],"change_assessment":"The gold should identify whether any reviewed rule materially changed between the comparison dates. It must not manufacture a legal change. If domain review confirms that the core ownership, DEA signer, PIC-accountability, and minimum-necessary authority rules remained materially stable, say so and separately identify the post-incident Goldfinch control clarification as an internal change."},"core_relationships":["Franchise ownership or corporate title is not equivalent to being the licensed pharmacy entity, DEA registrant, PIC, pharmacist, authorized controlled-substance purchaser, workforce member, or authorized PHI user.","Each controlled-substance-handling retail pharmacy location requires the appropriate DEA registration; authority from another store is not portable merely because both stores share branding or ownership.","Schedule II orders require the registrant or a person holding valid DEA power of attorney to execute the applicable Form 222 or CSOS order. CSOS credentials are individual and may not be shared.","Schedule III–V orders do not generally use Form 222, but remain within the pharmacy registrant's controlled-substance, security, recordkeeping, and authorized-purchasing framework.","A controlled-substance power of attorney must exist before the signer acts, be retained with the required records, be checked for current scope, and cease to support ordering after revocation or relevant termination.","The PIC may delegate tasks where lawful, but regulatory accountability and professional judgment are not transferred to the business owner. An owner cannot direct unlawful practice or sign a PIC or pharmacist attestation.","Minnesota permits nonpharmacist ownership structures subject to pharmacy licensure and PIC requirements; this does not confer professional, DEA, or PHI authority.","North Dakota imposes pharmacist-ownership restrictions. Any statutory exception, grandfathered status, or board-approved structure is entity-specific and must not be assumed to transfer with a franchise or grocery ownership change.","An owner or entity representative may sign a business or licensing submission only when the form calls for that owner or applicant signature, the signer has authority for the entity, and the statement is truthful. This does not permit the owner to sign registrant, PIC, pharmacist, or authorized-purchaser attestations.","Ownership does not create a HIPAA right to browse patient-level data. Internal access depends on workforce status, assigned duties, permissible purpose, authorization, and minimum necessary access; external service access requires an appropriate legal relationship and remains function-limited.","Business-associate status or an agreement does not create blanket access unrelated to contracted services.","Owner oversight should default to reporting that avoids patient-level PHI where the business purpose can be met with aggregate, de-identified, limited, or otherwise minimized information.","Urgency, vendor deadlines, financial loss, or owner instruction cannot cure missing legal authority or credentials.","If authority cannot be confirmed, the operational result is stop, preserve limited relevant facts, escalate to the appropriate regulated function, and resume only after objective confirmation or approval of a lawful alternative."],"must_should_boundary":{"must":["Use only a current authorized signer and the signer's own credentials.","Do not access or disclose PHI without a permissible, role-based basis.","Do not allow owner direction to override PIC accountability or professional judgment.","Comply with the applicable state's ownership, licensure, permit, and change-approval rules.","Stop and escalate when regulated authority cannot be confirmed."],"should_as_goldfinch_safeguards":["Default owner ordering authority and patient-level access to disabled until role approval is documented.","Independently verify a new signer or access grant before activation.","Periodically revalidate powers of attorney, CSOS access, role assignments, and owner access.","Use minimized business reports instead of patient-level data whenever they meet the oversight need.","Maintain a concise exception record showing what was requested, what authority was checked, who decided, and the disposition."],"labeling_rule":"The gold must not misstate these internal safeguards as universal statutory mandates, even when they are prudent enterprise controls."},"failure_paths":["No authorized Schedule II signer is present.","A CSOS certificate is expired, revoked, unavailable, or belongs to another person.","A power of attorney cannot be located or its current validity is uncertain.","An owner requests patient-level information but the role, purpose, workforce status, or disclosure basis is unclear.","A North Dakota ownership or control change may affect an exception or approved entity structure.","An owner and PIC disagree about a regulated action.","The normal process cannot be completed before an inventory or vendor deadline."],"source_families_to_validate":["Controlled Substances Act registration requirements and 21 CFR Part 1301, including separate registration principles","21 CFR Part 1305 provisions governing DEA Form 222, powers of attorney, and CSOS orders","HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules, including 45 CFR 164.502, minimum necessary provisions, workforce controls, safeguards, and business-associate limitations","Minnesota pharmacy statutes and rules governing pharmacy licensure, ownership or applicant disclosures, PIC responsibilities, and changes affecting a pharmacy license","North Dakota pharmacy statutes and administrative rules governing pharmacy ownership, permits, exceptions, PIC responsibilities, and ownership or control changes"],"dependent_outputs":{"owner_guide":"Carries the complete explanation, recommendation, period comparison, must-versus-safeguard distinction, state differences, authority rules, failure paths, and concise source support.","state_matrix":"Transforms the guide into action-by-state decisions. It must cover ownership or permits, who may sign, noncontrolled and controlled ordering, CSOS, power of attorney, PIC authority, PHI, business reporting, escalation, legal basis, and effective date.","exception_card":"Transforms the same rules into immediate stop, verify, escalate, preserve, and release instructions for frontline use when authority cannot be confirmed.","consistency_rule":"The matrix and card may simplify wording but may not broaden authority, omit a decisive state distinction, change a must into a recommendation, or contradict the guide's default model."},"sensitive_information_controls":["Do not include patient scenarios, names, prescription details, store credentials, DEA numbers, permit numbers, or copied signatures.","Use role and document categories rather than realistic patient-level examples.","Exception records should capture only the facts needed for compliance review and should never copy passwords, private keys, CSOS credentials, or unnecessary PHI."],"offline_build_validation":"A reference solution can be created and checked offline with a word processor, spreadsheet library, PDF renderer, and a pharmacist-maintained legal knowledge base current through the stated date. Render the PDF and DOCX, inspect page caps, parse the workbook, and walk every shared proposition across all three artifacts."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"owner_guide","filename":"franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"Plain-language franchise owner guide covering signing, ordering, information access, state differences, changes over time, and the recommended authority model.","required":true,"constraints":["No more than four rendered pages; current through October 1, 2025; compares the applicable position with January 1, 2024; excludes patient examples and software click-through instructions."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"state_matrix","filename":"state_authority_matrix.xlsx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet","description":"Usable state-by-state authority matrix supporting owner, PIC, controlled-substance ordering, licensing, and information-access decisions.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be readable as an operational matrix and remain consistent with the guide and exception card."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"exception_card","filename":"authority_exception_card.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"One-page frontline decision aid for situations in which authority, credentials, or permissible access cannot be confirmed.","required":true,"constraints":["Exactly one rendered page or less; must provide a safe stop-and-escalate path without software click-through instructions or patient examples."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"evaluate_compliance","work_product":"compliance_assessment","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"pharmacists","sector":"retail_trade","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":14.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference package must be authored by a pharmacist with retail controlled-substance compliance experience and independently reviewed by one pharmacy-law specialist and one privacy or health-information compliance reviewer. Reviewers must verify the federal and state authorities as of October 1, 2025, render every artifact, and perform a cross-deliverable consistency walk.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The package is an enterprise guide rather than legal advice for an unidentified store structure. North Dakota grandfathering or statutory exceptions and entity-specific board approvals cannot be resolved without store records, so the correct artifact must flag those cases for verification rather than presume eligibility. Contact names, local escalation numbers, and store-specific DEA or permit identifiers are intentionally unavailable."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"结构/材料验收记录","detail":"validated 属于结构、防泄漏、材料推进验收；没有内容正确率结论。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:03:54+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:03:54+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:03:54+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:03:54+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T09:03:54+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_c1/runs/2f1ccbfc69970f9b","declared_deliverables":["franchise_owner_authority_guide.pdf","state_authority_matrix.xlsx","authority_exception_card.docx"]};
